[[Lore:Thurvokun|Thurvokun]] was a [[Lore:Dragon|dragon]] whose name means "Overlord-Shadow" in the [[Lore:Dragon Language|Dragon Language]].<noinclude>

Thurvokun was among the dragons revered during the age of the [[Lore:Dragon Cult|Dragon Cult]] in [[Lore:Skyrim|Skyrim]], having a cult prior to the [[Lore:Dragon War|Dragon War]].{{ref|name=ESOUlfnor|[[ON:Ulfnor|Ulfnor]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} As a Dragon Lord, he chose his [[Lore:Dragon Priest|Dragon Priest]] {{Lore Link|Zaan}} when she was uncommonly young. It is said that her connection to Thurvokun was particularly strong, and it was speculated that their connection was spiritual, magical, or perhaps merely political. Whatever the case, Zaan grew a rapid and robust attachment, which her followers took as a sign of great fortune, and for around a decade, all was well.{{Ref|name=MtC:JD|{{Cite Book|Meet the Character - Jorvuld Davaux}}}}{{Ref|name=THotZS|{{Cite Book|The History of Zaan the Scalecaller}}}}

Thurvokun and his followers left central [[Lore:Skyrim|Skyrim]] for the remote mountain now known as {{Lore Link|Scalecaller Peak}}. It is believed that a schism in dogma within the Dragon Cult prompted their departure from Skyrim.{{Ref|name=AMOVAB|{{Cite Book|A Matter of Voice and Brass: Dragon Bones DLC Interview|ns_base=GEN}}}} Thurvokun eventually abandoned his worshipers, leaving Scalecaller behind and relocating to [[Lore:Fang Lair|Fang Lair]], for reasons unknown to all except for, perhaps, Zaan.{{Ref|name=THotZS}} Following his departure, Zaan fell into a depressive state,{{Ref|name=THotZS}}{{Ref|name=ABP|{{Cite Book|A Brother's Plea}}}} and her followers, who initially hoped Thurvokun would return, killed Zaan out of rage after many years of waiting.{{Ref|name=THotZS}}{{Ref|name=ASR|{{Cite Book|A Sister's Regret}}}} They attributed Thurvokun's departure to Zaan's weakness.{{Ref|name=THotZS}}{{Ref|name=ASRetort|{{Cite Book|A Sister's Retort}}}}

Thurvokun came to reside in the [[Lore:Dwemer|Dwarven]] city of [[Lore:Arkngthamz-Phng|Arkngthamz-Phng]] in the [[Lore:Dragon's Teeth Mountains|Dragon's Teeth Mountains]], which was rich with resources and life, as it was originally built by the Dwarves of [[Lore:Kragen|Kragen]]. It is believed Thurvokun found it to have more well-appointed accommodations than those chiseled out by the Nords in the service of the Dragon Priesthood.{{Ref|name=AP|{{Cite Book|Arkngthamz-Phng (book)}}}} Legend has it that when the Dragons and Dwemer still walked the land (at some point before {{Year|1E 700}}) Thurvokun drove the Kagren clan from Arkngthamz-Phng, and took the Lair for himself.{{ref|name=Silmane|[[Arena:Ria Silmane|Ria Silmane]]'s dialogue in [[Arena:Arena|''Arena'']]}} When they clashed, the Dwarves unleashed their greatest works in defense of their home, but they were no match for the raw power of the Dragon.{{Ref|name=ESOYisareh|[[ON:Yisareh|Yisareh]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} The bones of the thousands Thurvokun slaughtered in the city remained for ages, depicting a record of extinction and terror.{{Ref|name=JoOtB|{{Cite Book|Journal of Orryn the Black}}}} His roost came to be called "Fang Lair" by the locals,{{Ref|name=ESOYisareh}} a truncated adulteration of the original "Phng" paired with a suitably sinister descriptor for Thurvokun.{{Ref|name=AP}} In the ''[[Lore:King Edward|King Edward]]'' series, a golden dragon inhabiting Fang Lair had a large treasure trove of gold and absorbed the metal from it into his scales when lying on it. He encountered [[Lore:Moraelyn|Moraelyn]] and allowed him to take an ebony sword after they removed a cursed tooth from his mouth, and called himself "[[Lore:Akatosh|Akatosh]]" when asked for his name.{{Ref|name=KEVII|{{Cite Book|King Edward, Part VII}}}}

Thurvokun managed to live for millennia after the Dragon War because his reclusive hideaway kept him safe.{{Ref|name=ESOUlfnor}} Although he outlived many of his kin that met their end at the hands of [[Lore:Reman|Reman]]'s [[Lore:Dragonguard|Dragonguard]],{{Ref|name=ESOUlfnor}}{{Ref|name=Paarthy|[[Skyrim:Paarthurnax (dragon)|Paarthurnax]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} a group of adventurers found Fang Lair in the waning years of the [[Lore:First Era|First Era]] and traveled there to slay him.{{Ref|name=ESOUlfnor}}{{Ref|name=ESOCaluurion|[[ON:Caluurion|Caluurion]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Together, the warriors Ulfnor and Sabina Cedus, and mage Caluurion battled Thurvokun. Together they managed to kill Thurvokun, but Ulfnor and Sabina were crushed by his death throes.{{Ref|name=ESOUlfnor}} Caluurion attempted to capture his soul using a powerful [[Lore:Soul Trap|soul trap]], but he was unable to obtain it because of its divine properties. In an attempt to harness its power, he preserved many of Thurvokun's organs, and after his food supplies spoiled, turned to eating roast meat from the dragon's flesh. Caluurion remained in the lair as a [[Lore:Lich|lich]] and continued to try and harness the power of the dragon and the leftover [[Lore:Aetherium|aetherium]] crystals.{{Ref|name=ESOCaluurion}}{{Ref|name=CN|{{Cite Book|Caluurion's Notes}}}}

By the mid-[[Lore:Second Era|Second Era]], the legend of Thurvokun's triumph over the Dwarves remained a popular tale, and the researcher {{Lore Link|Neramo}} paid a visit to the ruins to document them in a [[Lore:Arkngthamz-Phng (book)|volume of Dwarven history]], which was later discovered by the Blackmarrow necromancer Orryn the Black.{{Ref|name=AP}}{{Ref|name=JoOtB}} Upon learning of the necropolis he and his followers traveled there in {{Year|2E 582}}. He questioned Caluurion's lich about Thurvokun, thralling and resurrecting him and his companions.{{Ref|name=JoOtB}} As Thurvokun's soul could not be obtained and bound, the necromancers sought alternatives to resurrecting the dragon, developing a method of binding the soul of a similar animal (such as a [[Lore:Mammoth|mammoth]] or [[Lore:Wamasu|wamasu]]){{Ref|name=RSC|{{Cite Book|Reanimation Specimen Collection|ns_base=ON}}}} to Thurvokun's skeleton in order to control it.{{Ref|name=REF|{{Cite Book|Reanimation Experiment Findings|ns_base=ON}}}}{{Ref|name=DiNLM|{{Cite Book|Death is No Laughing Matter|ns_base=ON}}}} A group of [[Lore:Undaunted|Undaunted]] warriors and Trailblazer Yisareh entered the ruins and fought off the necromancers and undead, freeing Caluurion, Ulfnor, and Sabina, who then helped them defeat Orryn's minions. Orryn the Black made his last stand by resurrecting Thurvokun's [[Lore:Skeletal Dragon|skeleton]] and controlling it with another animal's soul, later transporting his own soul into the bones when he was killed, but was defeated in the end. Yisareh then purged Orryn's soul from Thurvokun's bones, leaving the remains as they were prior.{{ref|name=ESO|[[ON:Casting the Bones|Casting the Bones]] quest in [[ON:Online|ESO]]}} Fang Lair remained an abandoned site in the [[Lore:Third Era|Third Era]], with the tale of Thurvokun living on.{{Ref|name=Arena|[[Arena:Fang Lair|Fang Lair]] in ''[[Arena:Arena|Arena]]''}}

Due to Caluurion's failure to capture Thurvokun's soul,{{Ref|name=CN}}{{Ref|name=ESOCaluurion}} it can be assumed that the dragon has been permanently destroyed and cannot be raised again. Although a dragon's soul generally persists after death unless consumed by a fellow dovah, the link between a dragon's soul and its physical remains is far stronger than that of a mortal. The effects of prying a dragon's soul from its body through necromantic act are therefore the subject of fierce scholarly debate, with some speculating that Thurvokun's soul once severed may have simply dissolved over time or returned to join his father [[Lore:Akatosh|Akatosh]].{{ref|{{Cite Book|Loremaster's Archive - Dragons in the Second Era}}}}

